# A pki block plus environment.looking-glass.certificate referencing one of its
# entries parses and validates. spec-lg-pki-certificate AC-1: the looking-glass
# HTTPS listener can be pointed at an operator certificate held in the PKI store,
# the same way the web listener already can.
#
# The looking glass is the surface a stranger visits, so the reference has to
# survive the whole config path rather than only the parser: a leaf that
# validates but never reaches the listener's own node would leave the operator
# reading a green "configuration valid" over a self-signed listener.
stdin=config:terminator=EOF_CONF
pki {
	ca lg-ca {
		certificate 
	}
	certificate public-lg {
		certificate 
		private {
			key MHcCAQEEIJWSrBva0yYuEY9NyV6LSqFoZ4eRNSoFUCZj9KdmaPuioAoGCCqGSM49AwEHoUQDQgAE9ujhR+AuyNpjClq9wS3fPg4sLDh2JYdiXLRih2EDVDNJAhFQEQ6RbqhwdSMnZUmPBBuoNbt1T6U+jcrRyF0VhA==
		}
	}
}

environment {
	looking-glass {
		enabled true
		certificate public-lg
	}
}
EOF_CONF

cmd=foreground:seq=1:exec=ze config validate -:stdin=config
expect=exit:code=0
expect=stdout:contains=configuration valid
# Validation says the file parsed. It does not say the reference reached the
# looking glass's own node, so read it back: `config show` prints the tree the
# rest of the system consumes, and the assertion fails if the certificate leaf
# stops landing under environment.looking-glass.
cmd=foreground:seq=2:exec=ze config show - environment looking-glass:stdin=config
expect=exit:code=0
expect=stdout:contains=certificate public-lg
